# Meta — health, version, the service root, and the admin's status check.
#
# The first four need no token. Start with these: if `/health` reports the
# database as down, every other file in this directory will fail in ways that
# look like bugs in the endpoint rather than in the connection.

### The service root. Says what this is and where the docs are.
GET {{baseUrl}}/

### Liveness, and whether MongoDB is actually reachable.
# 200 with "status": "ok"       -> database up
# 200 with "status": "degraded" -> the API is running, the database is not
GET {{baseUrl}}/health

### Version and environment. Worth checking before anything destructive:
# this is what tells you which environment you are actually pointed at.
GET {{baseUrl}}/version

### The OpenAPI document. Development only — 404 in production, by design.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api-json

### The Scalar API reference. Development only, same as above.
GET {{baseUrl}}/docs

### --- /status: everything above, plus the database, admin only ---

# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "{{adminEmail}}", "password": "{{adminPassword}}" }

###

@accessToken = {{login.response.body.$.access_token}}

### What the admin dashboard's header reads: environment, version, the
# database with credentials stripped, and whether /maintenance is registered
# on this deployment.
GET {{baseUrl}}/status
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### Without a token -> 401 missing_token.
GET {{baseUrl}}/status

### A token that is not an admin's -> 403 insufficient_role.
GET {{baseUrl}}/status
Authorization: Bearer not-a-real-token
